  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nWednesday through Saturday\n\nWe should see a break in the rain Wednesday afternoon into \nThursday morning. However, continued high moisture across the \narea with another weak wave means there will be another bout of \nshowers and storms during the day Thursday with a 70-90% chance \nareawide. No severe weather is anticipated. Then as the upper- \nlevel trough moves east, the surface low north of the Great \nLakes will bring a strong cold front through the area late \nThursday evening. This will bring some below-normal temperatures\nas well as gusty winds. These increased winds, despite the \ncooler temps, will bring an enhanced risk for agricultural \nfires. \n\nThere is quite the spread in LREF 850mb temperatures associated with \nthe cold front, with the 75th-25th percentile range being 5C to -2C. \nThis means there is some uncertainty about the extent of the frost \npotential Friday night into Saturday morning. Areas north of Highway \n30 have the highest potential of seeing temperatures get below 36F \nFriday evening, especially with clear skies and a calm wind \nforecast.  \n\nSaturday night through Sunday\n\nWinds will shift back to the southwest as high pressure drifts \neast, bringing slightly warmer temperatures for Sunday and \nMonday. Both the GFS and ECMWF have a shortwave moving through \nMonday morning in the southwest flow aloft, which could bring \nchances for rain, but that potential will need to be monitored.",
